  "worked_solution": "First, we find the roots of the characteristic polynomial:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda +3)}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have a repeated root $\\lambda =-3,$ so we have the following general solution:\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nWe find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions. \n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA=-2.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=2,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n-3A+B=2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $A=-2$ gives $B=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{-3x}-4x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$",
